WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.280 --> 00:00:04.160
The man who's too busy trying to solve
the world's problems

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:08.000
likely deserves a vacation. Yes. Yet the
number of times

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:11.519
Elon Musk has kicked up his feet during
his entire adult life

00:00:11.519 --> 00:00:15.679
can pretty much be counted on one hand.
And when he has tried to go on vacation

00:00:15.679 --> 00:00:19.680
it hasn't always worked out. While on his
honeymoon with his first wife Justine to

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:23.439
catch the 2000 Olympics,
he got some bad news as soon as they

00:00:23.439 --> 00:00:27.519
landed at Sydney airport.
The board of X.com, the precursor to

00:00:27.519 --> 00:00:32.640
PayPal, had decided to fire him as CEO, replacing him with Peter Thiel. So much

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:35.920
for the honeymoon.
He flew back to Palo Alto, California to

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:39.200
fight to get his job back
to no avail. He tried to go on his

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:45.120
honeymoon again three months later.
This time, he nearly lost his life.

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:48.960
While at a game reserve in his native
South Africa, Musk contracted the

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:52.879
deadliest form of malaria
and didn't even know it. When he returned

00:00:52.879 --> 00:00:56.559
to the US and fell ill,
doctors misdiagnosed him and he nearly

00:00:56.559 --> 00:01:01.120
died. By sheer luck, a visiting doctor who
happened to have experience with malaria

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:03.680
cases
saw his blood work, suspected he had

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:06.799
malaria, and immediately ordered
antibiotics.

00:01:06.799 --> 00:01:11.119
Musk got the treatment just in time. As
Ashlee Vance details in his book about

00:01:11.119 --> 00:01:13.920
the entrepreneur,
the doctor told musk that if he had

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:17.200
turned up a day later
the medicine likely would no longer have

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:20.320
been effective.
Musk later told Vance: that's my lesson

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:24.640
for taking a vacation.
vacations will kill you. He spent 10 days

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:29.119
in the intensive care unit
and took six months to recover, losing 45

00:01:29.119 --> 00:01:33.360
pounds.
And the curse continued. When he took a

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:37.520
week off in October 2014,
an unmanned Orbital Sciences Corporation

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:41.759
rocket exploded seconds after liftoff.
It was supposed to carry food, water, and

00:01:41.759 --> 00:01:44.320
supplies to the International Space
Station.

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:48.720
It wasn't his own rocket but it was
perhaps a sign of things to come.

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:52.880
And sure enough, the next time he tried
to take vacation his SpaceX rocket

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:55.680
exploded.
The lesson here is don't take a week off.

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:59.360
And Musk has pretty much lived up to
that promise with few exceptions.

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:03.119
He claims he doesn't have time to slow
down - not even on his birthday.

00:02:03.119 --> 00:02:06.719
When he turned 47, he was hard at work at
Tesla's factory.

00:02:06.719 --> 00:02:10.560
In fact, he stayed there all night. In a
previous video, I talked about how he's

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:14.920
been known to sleep at work.
That year in particular, 2018, was

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:19.440
excruciatingly painful.
He put in 120 hour work weeks as the

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:23.599
electric automaker struggled to mass
produce its most affordable vehicle yet,

00:02:23.599 --> 00:02:27.920
the Model 3. Taking time off as his
employees sweated on the factory floor

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:31.760
was out of the question,
explaining to CBS: I don't believe people

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:36.000
should be experiencing hardship while
the CEO is, like, off on vacation.

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:39.519
Tesla got over that painful period but
Musk isn't slowing down

00:02:39.519 --> 00:02:43.519
as he juggles several companies. He
recently revealed that his brain machine

00:02:43.519 --> 00:02:46.640
interface startup
Neuralink implanted a chip inside a

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:49.200
monkey's brain
that he says allowed it to control a

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:53.280
video game with its mind.
The Boring Company opened new offices in

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:58.159
Texas as its tunnel projects expand.
A day in the life of Elon Musk involves

00:02:58.159 --> 00:03:02.159
back to back
to back meetings and not casual ones as

00:03:02.159 --> 00:03:04.640
he recently explained on the new social
media app

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:08.000
Clubhouse. My days are, like, insane
torrents of information.

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:10.720
The meetings that are scheduled are not,
like, nice to have meetings. They're, like,

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:13.440
this meeting is essential.
It's pretty intense. When someone on the

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:17.280
question and answer website Quora
asked how they could be like Elon Musk,

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:21.599
an unlikely person responded.
His ex-wife Justine who was married to

00:03:21.599 --> 00:03:24.879
him for eight years
and had a front row seat inside the life

00:03:24.879 --> 00:03:28.080
and mind of one of the most successful
people in the world.

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:32.159
She wrote: Extreme success...comes at the
cost of many other things.

00:03:32.159 --> 00:03:36.159
Happiness is more or less beside the
point. And the essential ingredient for

00:03:36.159 --> 00:03:39.840
extreme success
is to be obsessed, be obsessed, be

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:42.799
obsessed.
In this case, nothing less than

00:03:42.799 --> 00:03:45.280
transitioning the world to sustainable
energy

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:49.280
and building civilizations in space. If we
are able to increase the rate of

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:53.519
innovation,
then life can become multi-planetary.

00:03:53.519 --> 00:03:57.280
This is the goal we should strive for.
While Musk is reluctant to take time off,

00:03:57.280 --> 00:04:00.480
many other successful people make it a
point to get away.

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:04.560
Richard Branson, the billionaire founder
of Virgin Group, has been known to relax

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:09.280
on his private Caribbean island. Netflix
CEO Reed Hastings swears by six weeks

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:11.760
off
every year, saying he often does his best

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:14.239
thinking when he's hiking in a mountain
somewhere.

00:04:14.239 --> 00:04:17.840
It's hard to imagine a scenario where
Musk takes even one week off,

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:22.160
let alone six, though at the rate he's
going he admits it might be high time

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:25.600
for some me-time. I was thinking, like, man,
how long can i keep this up?

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:28.800
I don't want my brain to explode. I was
thinking, like, maybe I should, like, at

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:30.720
some point, like, take a week off or
something.

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:34.160
Clear my mind. That won't involve
lounging around at the beach, however,

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:36.960
which he once said will leave him super
duper bored.

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:41.280
More like walking outside of a flying
plane which he did in England in 2015

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:45.440
with his second wife Talulah Riley.
If and when he does decide to finally

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:48.880
take a real vacation again,
it might just be aboard a certain

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:53.280
Starship on a journey out of this world.
That would be an extended vacation

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:56.479
because the trip to Mars would take
about six months each way.

